RRT: Pseudocode
def rrt(start, goal, obstacles, delta=0.5, max_iter=5000, goal_bias=0.05):
    tree = {start: None}           # node -&gt; parent

    for i in range(max_iter):
        if random() &lt; goal_bias:
            q_rand = goal          # occasionally aim at goal
        else:
            q_rand = random_config()  # otherwise explore

        q_near = nearest(tree, q_rand)      # closest tree node
        q_new = steer(q_near, q_rand, delta)  # one step toward sample

        if collision_free(q_near, q_new, obstacles):
            tree[q_new] = q_near   # keep only valid edges

            if (distance(q_new, goal) &lt; delta and
                collision_free(q_new, goal, obstacles)):
                tree[goal] = q_new  # connect final edge
                return reconstruct_path(tree, goal)

    return None                    # iteration budget exhausted

Walk through each function: random_config() returns a uniformly random point in C-space bounds. nearest() finds the closest tree node by Euclidean distance (or C-space metric). steer() moves from q_near toward q_rand by exactly delta distance (or less if q_rand is closer). collision_free() checks if the straight-line segment is obstacle-free (typically by sampling points along the segment). Goal biasing is a crucial practical trick: with probability 5%, we sample the goal itself as q_rand, which occasionally tries to grow the tree directly toward the goal, dramatically speeding up convergence.
